        if(f >= 0) {
            read(f, &c, 1);
            close(f);
        }
        return 0;
    }

Answer:
If the abc is a regular file then the duration of theprogram is as much as it takes to read the content from the file. During the I/O the process will be put in the WAITING state and after the I/O finises it will run again.
However, if abc is a FIFO for example, then we will need to add to the duration of the program how much it takes until the other end of the FIFO is opne in O_WRONLY in another process. If the other end won t be ever open, then the program will run to infinite.
